# Dédalo nightly backup — reference timer unit (audit S2-35; rebuilt for P0-13 on
# 2026-08-30).
#
# It copies the FIVE stores engineering/PRODUCTION.md §6 names — the matrix
# Postgres DB, the RAG pgvector DB (if enabled), the media originals, ../private/
# (.env secrets, session store, ts_state.json), and every site-builder INSTANCE on
# this host. One ExecStart per store, in the same order and with the same tokens as
# that table; test/unit/operator_commands_tripwire.test.ts holds the two equal, so a
# store documented there and copied by nothing is impossible.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THIS FILE BEFORE (P0-13 — every claim measured):
#
#   1. IT READ KEYS THE INSTALLER NEVER WRITES. $DB_PASSWORD/$DB_HOST/$DB_USER/
#      $DB_NAME/$MEDIA_PATH are the TS-native spellings; the wizard writes the
#      PHP-catalog ones (DEDALO_PASSWORD_CONN, DEDALO_HOSTNAME_CONN,
#      DEDALO_USERNAME_CONN, DEDALO_DATABASE_CONN — src/core/install/config_persist.ts),
#      and MEDIA_PATH it writes only when the CLI installer was given --media-path.
#      On a wizard-installed host those expanded to EMPTY STRINGS: a dump attempted
#      as the wrong role against the wrong host, into a file named after nothing.
#      The connection is now passed as the NAMES OF THE KEYS to read, and
#      deploy/dedalo-db-backup.sh resolves each one the way the engine's readEnv
#      does — TS-native name first, PHP alias second. It never guesses a value it
#      cannot resolve; it refuses, loudly, naming the key.
#
#   2. `Type=oneshot` WITH FOUR PLAIN ExecStart LINES STOPS AT THE FIRST FAILURE.
#      A database that was down at 03:30 meant the media originals, ../private/ and
#      every museum's site-builder instance were not copied either. Each store now
#      runs through deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh, whose header explains the shape:
#      stores 1..4 are `-`-prefixed so their exit status cannot cancel the others,
#      the LAST store carries `--last` and aggregates every recorded status, and the
#      unit FAILS if any store failed. Nothing is papered over: the difference
#      between "a store failed" and "the unit succeeded" is exactly what this
#      arrangement removes.
#
#   3. THERE WAS NO OnFailure= AND NO NOTIFICATION ANYWHERE IN deploy/. A backup
#      that fails silently every night is worse than no backup, because it is
#      believed — the maintenance panel reports a fresh artifact and the operator
#      authorises a code update on the strength of it. See
#      dedalo-backup-alert@.service and deploy/dedalo-backup-alert.sh.
#
#   4. THE MEDIA JOB WAS `rsync -a --delete` INTO ONE GENERATION. A mass deletion
#      was propagated into the only copy of the store PRODUCTION.md §6 calls "the
#      source of truth every derivative rebuilds from" within 24 hours.
#      deploy/dedalo-tree-backup.sh keeps dated, hard-linked generations and states
#      its retention rule (14 by default) in the file.
#
#   5. THE DUMP WAS NEVER READ BACK. deploy/dedalo-db-backup.sh dumps to a `.part`
#      name, reads the archive back end to end with pg_restore, and only then puts
#      it under a `*.backup` name — the suffix newestBackupMtimeMs() scans. A
#      truncated or still-running dump can therefore no longer be the freshest
#      "backup" on disk, which is what made the update panel say "ready".
#
# STORE 5 IS A SCRIPT, not a fifth inline command, because it is the one member
# whose sources are not known until the host is read: a host carries N museums, each
# declaring its own roots and its own sites. deploy/dedalo-site-builder-backup.sh
# reads those paths out of the artifacts the provisioner generated for each
# instance, so the backup cannot derive the host layout a second time and be wrong
# about it. On a host with no site builder it prints one line and exits 0.
#
# Enabled by dedalo-backup.timer. VERIFY RESTORES PERIODICALLY: reading a dump back
# proves the bytes are a complete archive, not that it restores into a working
# catalogue. Nothing but an actual restore proves that (PRODUCTION.md §6: quarterly).
#
# Canonical host layout (engineering/PRODUCTION.md): /opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/
# (the repo), /opt/dedalo/private/ (../private), user/group dedalo. Adjust the
# example /opt/dedalo/... paths below to the real host layout if different.

[Unit]
Description=Dedalo nightly backup set (matrix DB + RAG DB + media + private + site-builder instances)
# %n is this unit's own name, so the alarm can quote the right journal.
OnFailure=dedalo-backup-alert@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=DEDALO_USER
EnvironmentFile=/opt/dedalo/private/.env
# Where the per-run store statuses live. systemd creates it before the first step
# and REMOVES it when the unit stops, so one run can never read another's leftovers.
RuntimeDirectory=dedalo-backup
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0750
# Where the PERSISTENT verdict lives (BACKUP_FAILED / LAST_OK). It must match
# dedalo-backup-alert@.service's own Environment= line: both write BACKUP_FAILED and
# only a fully successful run removes it.
Environment=DEDALO_BACKUP_STATE_DIR=/opt/dedalo/backups
# A dump and a media mirror are a complete, unprotected copy of the collection.
UMask=0077

# 1. Matrix DB — custom format with blobs, read back with pg_restore before it is
#    allowed to be named `*.backup`. --dir-key follows DEDALO_BACKUP_DIR when the
#    operator moved the dumps, because that is the directory the panel lists and the
#    updater's freshness gate scans. NO --socket-key here on purpose: the wizard
#    writes DEDALO_SOCKET_CONN but the TS engine reads it NOWHERE (config.ts builds
#    the pool from DB_HOST/DB_PORT; src/config/migration_map.ts classifies the key
#    as read by nothing), and a backup that connects by a route the engine does not
#    use is a second, disagreeing answer to "which server holds the catalogue".
ExecStart=-/opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh run matrix_db /opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-db-backup.sh --label timer --dir-key DEDALO_BACKUP_DIR --dir /opt/dedalo/private/backups/db --db-key DB_NAME --host-key DB_HOST --port-key DB_PORT --user-key DB_USER --password-key DB_PASSWORD --pg-dump pg_dump --pg-restore pg_restore
# 2. RAG pgvector DB — UNCOMMENT when the semantic index is enabled. Its keys fall
#    back to the main database's in the ENGINE, but not here on purpose: a backup
#    that silently dumps the catalogue a second time because DEDALO_RAG_DB_NAME is
#    unset would look like a RAG backup and be none.
#ExecStart=-/opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh run rag_db /opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-db-backup.sh --label rag --dir /opt/dedalo/private/backups/rag --db-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_NAME --host-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_HOSTNAME_CONN --port-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_PORT_CONN --user-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_USERNAME_CONN --password-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_PASSWORD_CONN --socket-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_SOCKET_CONN --pg-dump pg_dump --pg-restore pg_restore
# 3. Media originals — dated generations, hard-linked to the previous one, 14 kept.
#    The source is read from MEDIA_PATH (or its PHP alias); an unset key is a loud
#    refusal, never a guess, because the engine's own default is one of two paths
#    and the wrong guess backs up an empty directory while reporting success.
ExecStart=-/opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh run media /opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-tree-backup.sh --label media --source-key MEDIA_PATH --dest /opt/dedalo/backups/media --keep 14
# 4. The private dir itself (secrets, sessions, state) — small, always, and
#    generational too: the .env is the file every other restore step reads first, so
#    yesterday's copy is worth exactly as much as the media's. Its own backups/
#    subtree is excluded (store 1 owns those artifacts).
#
#    HONEST LIMIT: the session store is a live SQLite database, so its copy can be
#    torn — a restore may lose logged-in sessions, which cost a re-login and nothing
#    else. The files that matter here (.env, ts_state.json) are written atomically by
#    the engine (tmp + rename), so they copy whole or not at all.
ExecStart=-/opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh run private /opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-tree-backup.sh --label private --source /opt/dedalo/private/ --dest /opt/dedalo/backups/private --keep 14 --exclude backups
# 5. Every site-builder instance: its declaration + secrets, its workspaces root,
#    each site's whole webspace (BOTH release stores and the two served symlinks),
#    and its audit trail. Exits 0 with one line on a host that declares none.
#
#    THE LEADING '+' RUNS THIS ONE STEP AS ROOT, ignoring User= above, and it is not
#    optional: an instance's secrets are 0600 root:root inside a 0700 root:root directory,
#    and its state roots are 0750 owned by that museum's own service user. DEDALO_USER is
#    in none of those groups — deliberately, that separation IS the isolation — so without
#    the '+' this step reads nothing and reports every museum as unbackuppable. The other
#    steps stay unprivileged.
#
#    IT IS ALSO THE LAST STEP, hence '--last': after copying its own store it
#    aggregates every store's recorded status and exits non-zero if ANY of them
#    failed. That is what makes the unit's verdict the truth about all five while
#    no single store can cancel another, and it is why this line alone is not
#    '-'-prefixed. If you comment this store out, move '--last' to the last
#    ExecStart that remains, or nothing will ever report the run's verdict.
#
#    The destination therefore holds root-owned 0600 credentials. Create it 0700 root:root
#    (`install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root /opt/dedalo/backups/site_builder`) and treat the
#    whole backup tree as secret material — which store 4 already made true.
ExecStart=+/opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh run --last site_builder /opt/dedalo/master_dedalo/deploy/dedalo-site-builder-backup.sh /opt/dedalo/backups/site_builder
